  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 345 AM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nThe 08Z/3 am surface map shows 1022-1024 mb high pressure across\nthe mid Atlantic States and ridging into the mid MS river\nvalley. 1002 mb low pressure was over central SD with a frontal\nboundary extending eastward over far southern MN into southern\nWI. South to SSE winds was starting to bring in warmer air\ninto central/se IL. Temps were in the mid 60s to lower 70s with\nmildest readings from I-55 west. Dewpoints were in the low to\nmid 60s. Patches of mid level clouds were over central IL and\nmore widespread over IL river valley and ne CWA. A 40-50kt sw\nlow level jet from OK Panhandle ne across central parts of KS\nand Iowa into southern WI was aiding in development of\nconvection over northern IL into southeast WI, far eastern Iowa\nand central/southern lower MI. Isolated light showers/sprinkles\nwere over the IL river valley with a heavier shower over far ne\ntip of Vermilion county east of Hoopeston. \n\nStrong mid/upper level ridge over the Southern Plains and\nmid/lower MS river valley to nose into central IL during today\nas 500 mb heights rise to 592-594 decameters by sunset. Latest \nCAMs show isolated convection over the IL river valley and north\nof I-74 today, mainly this morning. More widespread convection \nto be over northern/ne IL today. Most areas should continue to \nbe dry today. Increasing SSW winds to 10-15 mph and gusts 18-24 \nmph to bring warmer temperatures into the area, with highs in \nthe lower 90s, except upper 80s in cloudier areas from I-74 ne. \nMild lows overnight around 70F in eastern IL and lower 70s from \nI-55 west. \n\nStrong 500 mb ridge builds further north into IL on Mon as 500 \nmb heights rise to 594-595 decameters and continues holding \nfirmly in place Tue/Wed, keeping ring of fire of storms\ngenerally north of central IL. This will also bring hotter \ntemperatures into the area with highs Mon in the low to mid 90s \nand afternoon heat indices in the upper 90s to around 100F. \nHighs Tue-Thu in the mid to upper 90s with areas from \nSpringfield to Robinson sw approaching 100F. Heat indices Tue \nafternoon of 100-105, approaching 105 Wed afternoon and 100-105 \nThu and Fri afternoon. The long duration of anomalously warm \ntemperatures for early Sep and lack of overnight relief with \nlows in the 70s starts pushing the area into Major (Level 3 of \n4) to Extreme (Level 4 of 4) Heat Risk Tue-Fri, and remains \nelevated as the holiday weekend begins. Heat advisories will \nlikely be needed Tuesday through Friday for much of the area\nespecially if the stronger upper level ridge holds over the area.\n\nMedium range models continue to retrograde the strong mid/upper\nlevel ridge west/sw with IL getting into a nw upper level flow\nlate this week. Frontal boundary staying north of IL much of\nthis week, could start dropping southward into ne IL late this\nweek, and bring low convection chances into central IL Fri-\nSaturday. Temperatures trending a bit lower late this week with\nhighs Friday low to mid 90s, upper 80s/lower 90s Saturday and\nand Sunday. These temperatures are still above normal highs in\nthe low to mid 80s for early Sep. \n\nThe Climate Prediction Center (CPC) 8-14 Day Outlook for Sep\n6-12th has 55-65% chance of above normal temperatures with \n33-40% chance of below normal precipitation over central and \nsoutheast IL and near normal over nw/northern IL. So the very \nwarm and drier wx pattern looks to continue next two weeks.",
